00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.085
SPEAKER 1: Creating an
ML model in a notebook

00:00:02.085 --> 00:00:03.670
can be quick and
straightforward,

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:05.460
but when you need
to expand your ML

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:07.770
workflow to be
sustainable and scalable,

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:09.480
things can get more complex.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:12.060
In previous videos, we've
talked about pipelines and how

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.160
you can create,
manage, and reproduce

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.053
your workflows at a high level.

00:00:16.053 --> 00:00:17.970
Let's break it down by
walking through a model

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:20.314
that we'll later use in
a key Kubeflow Pipeline.

00:00:20.314 --> 00:00:22.734
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:27.640
Today, we'll go through the
details of a sample ML model.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:31.390
You can check out other Kubeflow
samples in the link below.

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:34.210
First, set up a notebook
server running on Kubeflow.

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:36.460
If you need a refresher,
check out our previous videos

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:37.900
on how to do that.

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.180
Next, clone the GitHub
repo linked below

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:43.090
and access the Chicago
Crimes Research notebook.

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:44.620
This notebook walks
through a model

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:47.110
that we'll use as a part
of a Kubeflow Pipeline

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:48.440
in the next video.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:51.340
So let's walk through how
the model actually works.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.710
The model uses Chicago
daily crimes data

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:55.780
to forecast how many
crimes are expected

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:59.260
to be reported the next day
based on how many were reported

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:00.940
over the previous days.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:02.560
Once the dependencies
are installed,

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.959
the first step is to query
the data table sitting

00:01:04.959 --> 00:01:07.180
in a bigquery for
daily crimes over time,

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:10.770
then download it into
our in Jupyter Notebook.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:14.070
Next, you can visualize the
data as a line plot named

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:16.740
Daily Crime Reports
to give you an idea

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.830
of the actual values over time.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.780
To prepare the data
for a model, you

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.460
need to pre-process the data.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.620
Since this is a
sequence model, you

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:29.370
need to split data sequentially
to avoid potential bias.

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.170
With time-based data,
you want to make

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.600
sure you're not feeding the
model data that happened

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.850
after the prediction window.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.070
Then, define the
percent of data you

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:40.710
should use for
your training, 80%,

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:42.840
and the number of
observations used to predict

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.280
the next observation.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:45.990
Our window size is seven.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:48.540
In other words, you'll be using
the prior seven days' worth

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:52.020
of data to predict the
next day's crime count.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.670
When we pre-process
the data, we'll

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:56.790
split it up into the training
and evaluation data sets

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:58.800
and then normalize
the data to avoid

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:00.870
the influence of any outliers.

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:02.760
In order to begin
training our model,

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.730
you need to reformat
our training data set.

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:07.830
This generates an
input function that

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:10.919
specifies features and
labels, whether to shuffle

00:02:10.919 --> 00:02:14.310
the data, number of
epochs, and batch size.

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:16.320
The resulting data set
can be fed directly

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.650
to the model for
training or evaluation.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:21.840
Then, create a
model for regression

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.270
which returns a compile
model, an object that we'll

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:25.950
use to train.

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:28.230
Now you can begin
training and evaluation

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:30.120
by passing in the
training data set,

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:34.400
validation data set, and finally
train the model using those.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.420
It returns the trained model.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.820
Once you specify batch size,
learning rate, and number

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:41.970
of epochs, you can run the
train and evaluate function

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:43.680
to get the final model.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.140
To evaluate the model,
take the trained model

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:49.870
and feed it the other
20% of evaluation data.

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:52.140
Finally, you can plot the
model's prediction outcome

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.240
and compare it to
the actual accounts.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.860
Looking pretty good.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.260
In the next video, we're
going to take this model

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.380
and create an ML application
using Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:02.550
Stay tuned.

